Title: "Set Theory"
Id: "4b9a150c-46bd-49da-83b4-b106f402c3a4"


Text: "The whole of the mathematics developed in these pages is built from a
       single kind of object -- the *set* -- and a single primitive relation
       between sets -- *membership*. Numbers, ordered pairs, functions, and the
       algebraic structures of later chapters are all, in the end, particular
       sets. This chapter fixes the rules that sets obey, in the form of the
       axioms of Zermelo and Fraenkel, and then uses those rules to construct
       the first sets we need."
Id: "a5b9d42b-f22f-4fd4-a867-29de537158c3"


Text: "A set is taken as undefined; the only thing one may ask of it is which
       sets belong to it. For sets $x$ and $X$, either $x \in X$ or
       $x \notin X$, and this membership relation carries all the information a
       set has to offer. The axioms below are collected as the defining
       conditions of what it means to be a set."
Id: "cbc6feb5-4f5e-41ef-bf59-342c981aa217"


[\set]
Declares: X
satisfies:
. \axiom.of.empty.set
. forAll: Y is \set
  then:
  . \axiom.of.extensionality#given{X := X; Y := Y}
  . \axiom.of.pairing#given{X := X; Y := Y}
. \axiom.of.union#given{X}
. \axiom.of.power.set#given{X}
. \axiom.of.infinity
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" X :-> \\abstract
Enables:
. from: Y ::= {y__ : ...}
  capability: x_ "in" X :-> x_ member_of Y
Documented:
. called: "set"
. description: "A set is the sole primitive object of the theory. The only
                question one may put to a set is which sets are its members, and
                two sets with the same members are the same set. Every other
                object built in this collection is ultimately a set."
Id: "a0759217-e1f6-412c-982a-0038cd17a3a1"


Text: "It is convenient to have notation for the failure of membership. Writing
       $x \notin X$ is nothing more than denying that $x \in X$."
Id: "48b22b9f-2acc-4e80-9148-667489d48ee1"


[x \.not.in./ X]
States:
when: x, X is \set
that:
. not: x "in"? X
Documented:
. written: "x? \notin X?"
. description: "The negation of membership: $x \notin X$ asserts that the set
                $x$ is not an element of the set $X$. It is shorthand for the
                statement that $x \in X$ fails to hold."
Id: "756e0f48-1c0e-4e05-b97a-9c4fcd577603"


SectionTitle: "The empty set"
Id: "8dc697a3-2ef0-4af9-8852-a994bdd8882d"


Text: "The theory needs at least one set to exist before it can build any
       others. The simplest such set is the one with no elements at all. Its
       existence is granted by the axiom of the empty set, and, as with every
       set, its uniqueness is a consequence of extensionality."
Id: "7a54c26a-ea2e-4e38-ac69-3426f651c4d4"


[\empty.set]
Defines: X is \set
expresses:
. not:
  . exists: Y is \set
    suchThat: Y "in" X
Documented:
. called: "empty set"
. written: "\emptyset"
. description: "The empty set $\emptyset$ is the unique set having no members
                whatsoever. It is the seed from which the entire universe of
                sets -- and eventually the natural number $0$ -- is grown."
Id: "96908ddf-5fc5-455d-9cfd-eaf0fd6ada38"


Text: "A set that is not the empty set has at least one element. It is worth
       naming this condition, since many constructions require a set to be
       nonempty."
Id: "6a19c255-0426-440b-a2e3-c97b1fe06bc4"


[\(nonempty)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies: X != \empty.set
Documented:
. adjective: "nonempty"
Id: "616950f0-7d70-48ff-a5ac-9d5d49272150"


SectionTitle: "The axioms"
Id: "f2e995e5-7f69-49c8-90bc-46d8f77811f1"


Text: "The following axioms are the rules a set must obey. The axiom of the
       empty set asserts that a set with no members exists, providing the
       starting point just discussed."
Id: "287b3b2f-e440-43b5-98be-b7310daf6aaf"


[\axiom.of.empty.set]
Axiom:
then:
. exists: X is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then:
    . not: Z "in"? X
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Empty Set"
. description: "There is a set having no members: for every set $Z$, $Z \notin
                X$. This is the one axiom that outright asserts a set into
                existence; every other set is built, directly or indirectly,
                starting from it."
Id: "f2180719-568d-4084-982b-a71c5f195bf6"


Text: "The axiom of extensionality fixes what it means for two sets to be equal:
       a set is completely determined by its members. Not the way it was
       described, not the order of its elements, and not any repetition -- only
       membership counts. This is what lets us speak of *the* empty set or *the*
       union of two sets."
Id: "7f76409d-9233-44bf-984a-ede2358dcee5"


[\axiom.of.extensionality]
Axiom:
given: X, Y is \set
where:
. forAll: Z is \set
  then: (Z "in"? X) \.iff./ (Z "in"? Y)
then: X = Y
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Extensionality"
. description: "Two sets are equal if and only if they have exactly the same
                elements. The identity of a set is therefore nothing more than
                the collection of its members, so any two constructions that
                yield the same elements yield the same set."
Id: "6fc3a89f-f8ef-420e-b1b6-6e5ed4905611"


Text: "The axiom of pairing lets us gather any two sets into a single set. From
       it, together with union, the finite sets and ordered structures used
       later can be assembled."
Id: "7b45fe82-a42a-4591-9453-350baec1df28"


[\axiom.of.pairing]
Axiom:
given: X, Y is \set
then:
. exists: U is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then: (Z "in"? U) \.iff./ ((.Z = X.) \.or./ (.Z = Y.))
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Pairing"
. description: "For any two sets $X$ and $Y$ there is a set $U$ whose members
                are exactly $X$ and $Y$. Applying it with $X = Y$ produces the
                set whose only member is $X$."
Id: "3219aca1-fba1-42a6-9a1f-209aa430c677"


Text: "The axiom of union collapses a set of sets into a single set containing
       every element of every member. It is what makes it possible to pool the
       contents of a whole family of sets at once."
Id: "c0f76644-e25f-4e0c-8cf0-6daf86ad59df"


[\axiom.of.union]
Axiom:
given: X is \set
then:
. exists: U is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: Z is \set
    then:
    . have: Z "in" U
      iff:
      . exists: W is \set
        suchThat:
        . W "in"? X
        . Z "in"? W
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Union"
. description: "For a set $X$ whose members are themselves sets, there is a set
                $U$ containing exactly those $Z$ that belong to some member $W$
                of $X$. It flattens one level of nesting, combining the elements
                of all the members of $X$."
Id: "60bcea09-6bf1-4917-b90f-64ceeb941558"


SectionTitle: "Subsets"
Id: "d1d71d30-1ce6-427e-bbc4-dd3ccf53fe53"


Text: "One set is a subset of another when every element of the first is also an
       element of the second. Subsethood is the basic way of comparing sets by
       inclusion, and the power set axiom that follows depends on it."
Id: "7bfbdad3-bf51-4e2d-ae06-8dd2fa3a220d"


[A \:set.theoretic.subset:/ B]
Declares: A is \set
when: B is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: X "in" A
  then: X "in"? B
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" A :-> x_ "in" B
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ is a subset of $B?$"
. written: "A? \subseteq B?"
. description: "$A \subseteq B$ holds when every element of $A$ is also an
                element of $B$. By extensionality, two sets are equal exactly
                when each is a subset of the other."
Id: "2b2b89a1-d106-4377-ae48-14c704063e5e"


Text: "A subset that is not the whole set is called a *proper* subset: it is
       contained in the larger set but differs from it."
Id: "c27fbf42-311e-4efd-b4c8-2da3ad6370a7"


[A \:set.theoretic.proper.subset:/ B]
Declares: A \:set.theoretic.subset:/ B
when: B is \set
satisfies: A != B
Documented:
. written: "A? \subsetneq B?"
. description: "$A \subsetneq B$ holds when $A$ is a subset of $B$ but is not
                equal to $B$; that is, $B$ has at least one element not in $A$."
Id: "7d070f37-61a5-4c62-b8da-4831c222e4b2"


Text: "The axiom of the power set asserts that all the subsets of a given set
       can themselves be gathered into a set. This is the first axiom that
       produces a set strictly larger than the ones it starts from."
Id: "139b6eca-d1e5-4fae-97a4-1b05d96726f1"


[\axiom.of.power.set]
Axiom:
given: A is \set
then:
. exists: P is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: x is \set
    then: (x "in"? P) \.iff./ (x \:set.theoretic.subset?:/ A)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Power Set"
. description: "For every set $A$ there is a set $P$ whose members are exactly
                the subsets of $A$. This power set collects together all of the
                ways of selecting elements from $A$."
Id: "8557cdb3-e02f-4b75-aed1-3dfd1cdee895"


SectionTitle: "Building new sets"
Id: "b49297d0-a6d3-4843-85a0-2241c30b5a05"


Text: "With the axioms in place, we can name several standard constructions. The
       singleton of a set is the set whose only member is that set, obtained
       from pairing."
Id: "032576ab-7fc3-4075-b24a-cd06583d18cd"


[\singleton.set:of{X}]
Defines: Y is \set
when: X is \set
expresses:
. let: Z is \set
  then:
  . have: Z "in"? Y
    iff: Z = X
Documented:
. written: "\left \{ X? \right \}"
. description: "The singleton $\{X\}$ is the set whose sole member is $X$: a set
                $Z$ belongs to it exactly when $Z = X$. Its existence follows
                from pairing applied to $X$ with itself."
Id: "08f35257-d0a7-42af-9866-7d138c56fc4c"


Text: "The successor of a set adjoins the set itself as a new element to its
       singleton. Iterating this operation from the empty set is precisely how
       the natural numbers will be built in a later chapter."
Id: "f49b05f5-b826-45d0-b327-b248946e28fc"


[\set.successor:of{X}]
Defines: Y is \set
when: X is \set
expresses:
. let: Z is \set
  then:
  . have: Z "in"? Y
    iff:
    . (Z = X) \.or./ (Z = \singleton.set:of{X})
Documented:
. written: "X? \cup \left \{ X? \right \}"
. description: "The successor of $X$ is the set $X \cup \{X\}$, whose members
                are the members of $X$ together with $X$ itself. Starting from
                $\emptyset$ and repeating this step generates the natural
                numbers."
Id: "002b9914-ad06-4f5a-a3fb-803bd86e6c4b"


Text: "The last axiom guarantees that this process never terminates: there is a
       set closed under successor and containing the empty set, hence an
       infinite set. Without it, only finite sets could be shown to exist."
Id: "9196ed0a-391f-4fb5-af13-ac8c65db0ae9"


[\axiom.of.infinity]
Axiom:
then:
. exists: I is \set
  suchThat:
  . oneOf:
    . \empty.set "in"? I
    . forAll: Y is \set
      then:
      . if: Y "in"? I
        then: \set.successor:of{Y} "in"? I
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Infinity"
. description: "There is a set $I$ that contains the empty set and is closed
                under the successor operation. Such a set cannot be finite, so
                this axiom is what first admits an infinite set into the theory."
Id: "a0f45971-fcc1-419b-bcd5-f4c4717bb37f"


Writing:
. "phi :~> \varphi"
. "Xb :~> \mathbb{X}"
Id: "c51b2bbc-d8f1-406c-b7c8-317e5409a2d8"


SectionTitle: "Set-builder notation"
Id: "55851b31-9fa9-4b8e-b61b-315b7559c66d"


Text: "Sets are most often described by a property their elements satisfy. The
       remaining definitions of this chapter set up that notation -- properties
       of sets, sets listed by their elements, ordered pairs, and the Cartesian
       product -- which is used throughout the rest of the collection."
Id: "b218d7dd-c01a-476d-9975-b05de57b8b8f"


[\set.property(X_)]
Declares: phi(X_) is (? is \set) -> (? is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "set property"
. description: "A set property is a predicate on sets: given a set it yields a
                statement, true or false. Properties are what the specification
                axiom uses to carve subsets out of existing sets."
Id: "cc680a81-3338-4848-8b9d-fcf368dd759e"


[\set:of{x[i_ := 1...n]}]
Defines: X is \set
when: x[1...n] is \\opaque
expresses:
. let: y is \\opaque
  then:
  . have: y "in"? X
    iff:
    . anyOf:
      . x[1...n] = y
Documented:
. called: "set of $\left \{ x? \right \}$"
. written: "\left \{ x? \right \}"
. description: "The set $\{x_1, \dots, x_n\}$ listed by its elements: a value
                belongs to it exactly when it equals one of the listed $x_i$.
                This is the finite, roster form of set-builder notation."
Id: "61c901bc-5f36-479b-a32c-c37b7f98a9fb"


Text: "From unordered pairs one can encode *ordered* pairs, in which the two
       components can be told apart. Kuratowski's device below records the first
       component as a singleton and the pair together, so that the order is
       recoverable."
Id: "da7d91b3-693b-4ca2-ac2a-e94555b18c5f"


[\set.pair:of{x, y}]
Defines: p := \set:of{x, \set:of{x, y}} is \set
when: x, y is \set
Documented:
. called: "pair of $x?$ and $y?$"
. written: "\langle x?, y? \rangle"
. description: "The ordered pair $\langle x, y \rangle$, encoded as the set
                $\{x, \{x, y\}\}$. This encoding lets the first and second
                components be recovered from the set alone, so order is
                meaningful even though sets are unordered."
Id: "8fd9d494-2882-4639-bb50-44a56626d1c4"


Text: "Subsethood is transitive: a subset of a subset is a subset of the whole.
       This is the first small theorem of the collection, and it follows
       directly from the definition of subset."
Id: "370b0f76-ab0f-4f7f-80fa-120bb7ffe9e2"


Theorem:
given:
. C is \set
. B \:set.theoretic.subset:/ C
. A \:set.theoretic.subset:/ B
then:
. A \:set.theoretic.subset?:/ C
Id: "6a4bcb50-97bc-4d1b-a9b2-750c963bb720"


Text: "The characterization of equality by mutual inclusion, promised when
       extensionality was stated, is recorded here as a theorem: two sets are
       equal exactly when each is a subset of the other."
Id: "a0b6d62f-1f1d-4be5-997b-40aa03cc4ac7"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then: A = B
iff:
. A \:set.theoretic.subset?:/ B
. B \:set.theoretic.subset?:/ A
Id: "d3ee9362-d0be-47f0-bf0e-ecea7416d100"


Text: "Finally, the Cartesian product forms the set of all ordered pairs drawn
       from two sets. It is the setting in which relations and functions are
       defined, and so is one of the most heavily used constructions in what
       follows."
Id: "331daec2-edc7-4316-8fb6-0c801c3692ed"


[A \.set.cross./ B]
Defines: X := \set@{(a_, b_) : a_ "in" A; b_ "in" B}
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "Cartesian product of $A?$ and $B?$"
. written: "A? \times B?"
. description: "The Cartesian product $A \times B$ is the set of all ordered
                pairs $(a, b)$ with $a \in A$ and $b \in B$. Relations on a set
                are subsets of its product with itself, and functions are
                relations of a special kind."
Id: "3bdd9cfb-f836-4d44-880a-873bb772cdef"
